      {
        "id": "p2-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the difference between a three-digit number and its reversal is always a multiple of 99.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$99(A-C)$, where $A$ and $C$ are the first and last digits.\n\nWrite both numbers using place value and extract the common factor 99.",
          "worked": "Let the three-digit number have digits $A$, $B$ and $C$. Its value is $100A+10B+C$ and its reversal has value $100C+10B+A$. Let their difference be $D$.\n$$\\begin{align*}D&=(100A+10B+C)-(100C+10B+A)\\\\D&=100A+10B+C-100C-10B-A\\\\D&=99A-99C\\\\D&=99(A-C).\\end{align*}$$\nSince $A-C$ is an integer, the difference is a multiple of 99.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p2-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that the difference between any positive integer and its reversal is always a multiple of 9.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The difference is $9k$ for some integer $k$.\n\nExpand both numbers using place value. Their digit sums cancel, and every remaining coefficient is a multiple of 9.",
          "worked": "Let the digits of the positive integer be $A_n,\\ldots,A_1$, from left to right. Let $D$ be the number minus its reversal.\n$$\\begin{align*}D&=(10^{n-1}A_n+\\cdots+A_1)-(10^{n-1}A_1+\\cdots+A_n)\\\\D&=10^{n-1}(A_n-A_1)+\\cdots+(A_1-A_n).\\end{align*}$$\nThe digit differences sum to zero:\n$$ (A_n-A_1)+(A_{n-1}-A_2)+\\cdots+(A_1-A_n)=0. $$\nSubtracting this zero sum gives\n$$ D=(10^{n-1}-1)(A_n-A_1)+(10^{n-2}-1)(A_{n-1}-A_2)+\\cdots+(10^0-1)(A_1-A_n). $$\nFor every positive integer $r$,\n$$10^r-1=9(10^{r-1}+10^{r-2}+\\cdots+1).$$\nAlso, $10^0-1=0$. Thus every term in the expression for $D$ is a multiple of 9, so $D=9k$ for some integer $k$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      }
    ]
  },
